on-arrival: {! Q.cyprus_campaign_report=AnatolianRules.cyprusCampaign.report(Q,22); Q.cyprus_atilla1_briefing = AnatolianRules.cyprusAtilla1.briefing(Q, 22); !}


= 22 July 1974 — UN Ceasefire!

In Cyprus, with the achievement of the junction, the pressure of the previous night had been relieved, most importantly, the Turkish forces had managed to enter the city by 11:00, with it the Greek line collapsed and both 251st and 306th battallions were forced to retreat from Kyrenia. Despite some Turkish tanks sustaining damage 241st battallion would also be pushed away and 306th battallion's captain would be captured. While the complete takeover of the city had not been achieved today, it was only a matter of time.

In the meantime, the completion of Junction saw the Airborne and Marine forces aiming at removing all resistance in the Beşparmak Mountains- however many had left by our prior operations. The General Staff was very pleased that our forces had managed to succeed in its achievements. With our military might been proven to the World, and the Second Wave was making land while our forces on field were making advances, the United Nations Security Council had commenced an emergency meeting the day we started our operations and now given us a demand of ceasefire.

It is not a possibility to refuse this ceasefire without utterly destroying our diplomatic stance. However, both we and UN knew that regardless of our actions the Greeks, or at the very least Cypriot militias, would continue their actions, and there was the serious question of the state of our military positions, safety of our soldiers and the very bare question of if we had done enough. Therefore, the most logical action appeared to be accepting the ceasefire while moving as much as possible to secure our flanks until the deadline, and keep responding and advancing whenever we enter into conflict with the enemy forces. Or we could also demonstrate our level headedness and confidence and halt all of our offensive movement, such action this early would only be sensible if we are truly confident of our military state and at our prospect of achieving a peaceful diplomatic resolution without any further military action.

- @historical: Accept the ceasefire and secure our flanks before the deadline
- @alternative: Accept the ceasefire and halt offensive movement immediately

